{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.BraidKnot.Li2qUniqueness","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.Li2qUniqueness","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.SpecialFunctions.Log.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.MeanValue","lean:module:Mathlib.MeasureTheory.Integral.IntervalIntegral.FundThmCalculus"],"declarations":6,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_unique_from_ftc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_unique_from_ftc","kind":"theorem","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L69-L83","doc":"**THEOREM (antiderivative uniqueness from FTC — derived).**\nTwo continuous functions `F, G` on `[0, V]` that are both right-derivatives\nof the *same* `f` on `[0, V)` and agree at the left endpoint `0` agree at\n`V`.  This is the rigorous mathematical core of the Li₂^q uniqueness\nclaim: the q-logarithm fixes `f`, the initial condition `F 0 = G 0` fixes\nthe constant, so the antiderivative is unique.  Discharged via Mathlib's\nmean-value FTC `eq_of_has_deriv_right_eq`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.briggs_klein_is_neg_li2q","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.briggs_klein_is_neg_li2q","kind":"def","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L52-L65","doc":"**The Briggs–Klein integral identity** (named conditional conjecture).\nBy FTC, if `F' = f` and `F 0 = 0` then `F V = ∫₀^V f`; with the\nsubstitution `u = −t` this reads `∫₀^V ln_q(1+t)/t dt = −Li₂^q(−V)`.\n\nThe statement: the Briggs–Klein chart integral `∫₀^V ln_q(1+t)/t dt`\nequals `−Li₂^q(−V)`.  Here `bkIntegrand` is the q-log quotient supplied\nby the caller and `li2qAntideriv` its claimed antiderivative; the\nproposition names the FTC integral equation honestly as a `Prop`\n(its formalisation needs the q-log change-of-variables, the open part\nper `prop:mass-endomorphism-tower`), rather than asserting `True`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_deriv_term","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_deriv_term","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L44-L49","doc":"**THEOREM.** Termwise derivative: d/dz [z^n/(n·[n]_q)] = z^{n-1}/[n]_q. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_at_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.li2q_at_zero","kind":"theorem","line":37,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L37-L42","doc":"**THEOREM.** Li₂^q(0) = 0 (initial condition). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.li2qTrunc","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.li2qTrunc","kind":"def","line":29,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L29-L35","doc":"The symmetric q-dilogarithm truncated at order N:\nLi₂^q_N(z) = Σ_{n=1}^{N} z^n / (n · [n]_q).\n-- Ref: [kirillov1995] §2.1 ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidKnot.symQInt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidKnot.symQInt","kind":"def","line":25,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/BraidKnot/Li2qUniqueness.lean#L25-L27","doc":"The symmetric q-integer [n]_q = (q^n - q^{-n})/(q - q^{-1}). ","signatureChunk":0}]}